  "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ then\n${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ each contain a row consisting only of zeros, so they are not invertible. However,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis invertible. So, $S$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, if $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix that is not invertible then $det(A)=0$ and therefore for all $\\alpha \\in R$ \n\n$$\ndet(\\alpha A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot det(A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $S$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $S$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"",
